inning of january that changed  the game and i'm 100 uh  confident that that was the right  decision when they gave us the data  and uh you know there was three things  that we we had to focus on  uh first of all we we saw the the spread  the positivity rate was 40 percent those  are staggering numbers four out of 10  people  were  getting omicron  now we we see it drop down to 15.9  percent so that that's positive we we  saw the cases of the hospitalizations  where they they were they were doubling  every single week now we're seeing that  ease and another big big factor was the  absenteeism of our healthcare workers  but not just our healthcare workers uh  other uh businesses right across the  province so we're we're seeing a  positive trend we're still very cautious  and i always say we aren't out of the  woods yet but we're taking a cautious  approach to move forward  and i'm confident  that we're going to  move forward cautiously and get things  back to normal as quickly as possible  dr moore do you have anything to add  no i think the premier's response was uh  absolutely right on um in october we had  no understanding uh that  omicron would be declared a new variant  of concern november 26th we had a good  plan in place for delta and thanks to  all ontarians they were  getting immunized they were following  all the basic public health measures to  ensure safety in our community so we've  responded to a new variant quite  appropriately sadly we've had to put  public health measures in play at  present to best protect ontarians in the  health care system uh again thank  sacrifices they've made it's made a  difference our health system has been  able to provide the right care at the  right time for any patient with covid19  certainly we're at capacity in the  healthcare system we've made sacrifices  for  elective surgeries to be able to provide  that capacity but all ontarians are  receiving the care that's required at  present and thank them once again for  their um for their sacrifices and  we have to be adaptive to uh any new uh  variant uh and ontarians have done that  um for omicron uh we do uh see light at  the end of this phase of the pandemic as  the premier has outlined  and we do see hope that the sacrifices  have been worth it by the businesses  affected  and like any public health measure they  have to have a time limited  place  and  now that we've  anticipated the health system being able  to provide the right care at the right  time for all patients in ontario we're  absolutely prepared to remove the  measures uh in a cautious and gradual  staged and phased approach  premier  the cfib says that one in five  businesses is on the verge of financial  ruin and collapse  i i know that you've already pointed to  some of the supports that have already  been presented but they are saying that  even with restrictions lifted consumer  confidence isn't there and they will  suffer going forward what more does your  government need to do  to add financial support to make sure  that they don't  collapse  well first of all my my heart breaks for  the businesses i'm i'm a business minded  person own a business i get it i  understand how tough it is to to meet  payrolls and meet the the sales coming  in and  and meeting all the challenges but we've  stepped up two tunes of billions and  billions of dollars we just put out a  ten thousand dollar grant we're also uh  making sure that they were taking care  of their electricity costs and other  areas of support from the federal  government as as well uh folks you know  when we get back out there i encourage  you to go in and support your local  business go go out to the restaurant  even at the the 50 please  take a night out at least once a week  and go to your local restaurant and  support them either take out or or  you're able to sit inside but make no  mistake about it i i i understand i  fully understand the pain they've gone  through  but we'll we'll come back we'll come  back strong  out of this  our economy is strong i'm going to say  it'll be like the roaring 20s  we've seen 160 000 more people employed  uh right as we speak right now than we  did prior to the pandemic  we've created the environment for for  companies to come from all over the  world and and open up businesses here uh  we we need more people  we need another 315 000 uh people to  fill the jobs  so i'm confident about the economy i'm  feeling very confident we're going to  cautiously open up and we're going to  get back to normal as soon as we  possibly can  i can tell the business owners  i i i can't stand closing them down i  know the doc knows that i know the  minister knows it  but health health is a priority and i  follow directions and advice from our  health team  and that's that's what was able to get  us  through the last few weeks  okay we're ready to move to the phone  lines for questions first question  please  from rob ferguson with the toronto star  please go ahead  hi premier all and or doctor moore i'm  just wondering um  since in in the past you've had to kind  of pause or delay some of these  reopenings i'm wondering what  specifically  you would be  looking at that would kind of be the  trip wire  for having to pause or temporarily halt  these kind of measures what are the key  things you're looking at i'll pass out  dr moore  thanks rob so just in particular it's  not one  number that we're monitoring uh it is a  series of data points uh as the premier  announced uh the percentage of tests  that are positive is on the decline at  15 percent so that is one indicator  we're following very closely as well the  ability to care for ontarians in the  intensive care unit setting as well as  in the hospital  thank you to all the health care workers  that continue to provide care we're at  100 capacity for the medical and  surgical beds but  we are providing care for covid patients  in the right place at the right time the  other metric is the number of outbreaks  in long-term care facilities in  particular because  patients have to move eventually from  the acute care sector back into  long-term care or from long-term care  back into the acute care sector so those  are some of the metrics that we're  following um as well as the ability to  care for individuals in the health  sector in the in the premier already  mentioned the number of people that are  not in the work  called in sick  we're seeing improvement in those  metrics and they're plateauing  in terms of the hospital use we  anticipate by the 31st that there will  be ongoing improvements but february  will be difficult as well we absolutely  anticipate the need to have continued  icu capacity for for patients uh and  hence uh the premier's call to continue  to get immunized to continue to come  forward for the third doses is uh will  be important every day going forward 11  days to the opening um and we want a  million more ontarians coming forward we  can immunize more than a hundred  thousand a day so important that anyone  with an underlying medical illness in  ontario get their third dose especially  those that are 50 and older and there  are appointments available across  ontario to do that over the next several  days so please take advantage of the  availability of vaccine  follow-up  yes thank you doctor you were mentioning  that everyone with kovitz getting the  care they need but a lot of people with  needing cancer and other  surgeries and heart procedures and  whatnot are are still on this  this uh pause when will you lift that so  that hospitals can get more and max back  to normal and  when do you think under the timelines  for reopening that you'll be able to get  back to  broader pcr testing than we're having  now was mostly limited to healthcare  workers and people at high risk  well it's very impor unfortunate that we  had to provide directive two to pause  non-emergent non-urgent surgeries if  people do have a life-threatening  condition