oportionate  and balanced to the risk the risk still  remains relatively high we  clearly are seeing a number of outbreaks  across long-term care facilities both  and positivity in workers as well as in  their clients and patients  uh and and i think it's important to  keep those restrictions that are in  place uh until we see uh  significant improvement uh in the  transmissibility and activity of omicron  in our community so please bear with us  thank you for the sacrifices you're  making we we know  these have an effect  on the well-being of some individuals in  these facilities  but we also need to balance  those with the risks of transmission in  those facilities so we  we review them on a regular basis uh and  ensure that we remove them in a timely  and  safe fashion  and meet with the ministry of long-term  care  on a regular basis  with a formal meeting every friday  afternoon to review all measures  we'll take our final question from the  floor  mr premier this is a question i've asked  um whenever there's a  trauma there's a question i've asked a  lot of premiers  right now there's a a incident uh  happening in brampton  and mississauga that um is very tragic  um  first of all your thoughts and then how  can you reach out to the uh community  that's traumatized right now yeah they  they just uh briefly literally before i  walked in the door my heart just breaks  you know for for the family and the  whole community what what a tragedy  it is and we'll we'll be there i'll  speak to the mayor patrick brown  uh today reach out to see if there's  anything uh we can do  but what what a loss  it's terrible absolutely terrible  thanks everyone okay thank you  you
 

